  What does matter: the interrupt stack
  - Bench test: I overflowed it deliberately. TF-M, the secure firmware, took a HardFault, printed FATAL ERROR: HardFault, and halted with interrupts off. Four seconds later the watchdog reset the unit and
    the next boot said plain wdt, with no fatal:, stall: or fz: note. That is the same signature as the car's three freezes. The console log is
    /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-mark-code-l0destar-firmware/e136f998-e444-481f-ba27-33c0140a0287/scratchpad/w3/console_ram.log.
  - Correction: Earlier I said a plain wdt meant no fault was taken. That was wrong. A HardFault handled by TF-M leaves exactly this signature. The freeze capture in 0.4.127 can't see it either; it printed
    nothing in this test.
  - Not proven for the car: On the bench the interrupt stack used at most 304 of 2048 bytes (that includes boot-time init), so it looks roomy. The car's real peaks are unknown.
  - Tightest margin measured: the log thread, at 604 of 768 bytes. An overflow there gives a noted fatal:2, not a hang.

  Proposed fix, not built
  1. Remember the interrupt stack's high-water mark across a reset and report it on the next boot. After the next hang it would show whether this stack was exhausted.
  2. Set CONFIG_TFM_HALT_ON_CORE_PANIC=n, so a secure-side fault reboots the unit at once instead of freezing for about 12 s.
  3. Optionally raise the interrupt stack to 3 KB, which costs 1 KB of RAM.

